CATEGORIES:Guided Walk,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260807T145517Z
DESCRIPTION:Champion #4\, 2015\, Archival pigment inkjet print on archival paper\, framed with museum glass\nJoin artist Prarthna Singh for a walkthrough of her work in Grip\, Hold\, Pin. Through photography\, Singh explores the intersections of gender\, identity\, and nationhood\, often turning her lens towards lives shaped by resilience and quiet acts of resistance.\n\nIn this exhibition\, she documents the lives of women wrestlers\, tracing their everyday routines\, relationships\, and aspirations beyond the arena. Her images reveal how strength is negotiated through the body\, labour\, and care\, while reflecting on the larger social and political landscapes that shape contemporary India.\n\nThis event is in conjunction with programming for Grip\, Hold\, Pin. \n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260816T070000Z
DTSTART:20260816T060000Z
SUMMARY:Artist Walkthrough - Prarthna Singh
UID:1374@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/artist-walkthrough-prarthna-singh/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/1000243457-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Performances,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260806T070745Z
DESCRIPTION:                                                  Tvarita Arts Collective India \n\nJarasandha Vadhe is a well-known story from the Mahabharata. To help Yudhishthira perform the Rajasuya Yajna\, Lord Krishna\, along with Bhima and Arjuna\, visits the powerful King Jarasandha of Magadha. A fierce duel follows between Bhima and Jarasandha\, ending with the victory of good over evil through Krishna's guidance and Bhima's strength.\n\nThis Yakshagana performance features four iconic characters - Krishna\, Jarasandha\, Bhima\, and Arjuna and brings the story to life through expressive acting\, traditional music\, vibrant costumes\, and energetic dance. Presented by Team Yakshadegula\, the performance is uniquely brought to life by an ensemble of women Yakshagana artists\, celebrating both the richness of this classical storytelling tradition and the growing presence of women in the art form. \n\nThe performance is curated by Tvarita Arts Collective India. \n\nThis event is in conjunction with programming for Grip\, Hold\, Pin. 
DTEND:20260816T140000Z
DTSTART:20260816T130000Z
SUMMARY:Jarasandha Vadhe - Yakshagana Performance
UID:1371@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/jarasandha-vadhe-yakshagana-performance/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Untitled-design-14-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Workshops,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260810T060109Z
DESCRIPTION:Sweat and Soil\, #008\, Indrajit Khambe\, 2018 Motibag Talim\, Kolhapur\, Maharashtra\, India 35.56 cm\, 53.34 cm\nJoin photographer Indrajit Khambe for a workshop exploring photography as a tool for observation\, storytelling and long-term engagement. Drawing from his practice documenting communities\, landscapes and cultural traditions across India—including his acclaimed series Sweat and Soil\, featured in MAP's exhibition Grip\, Hold\, Pin- Khambe will reflect on his creative journey and the making of sustained photographic projects. \n\nParticipants will explore the creative potential of mobile phone photography\, discuss its possibilities and limitations\, and learn practical approaches to building a meaningful photography practice.\n\nThis event is in conjunction with programming for Grip\, Hold\, Pin.
DTEND:20260815T083000Z
DTSTART:20260815T053000Z
SUMMARY:From Image to Practice
UID:1376@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/from-image-to-practice/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/DSC_0440-2-option-2-copy.jpg
CATEGORIES:Talks,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260806T072434Z
DESCRIPTION:The Street as Studio\, Cop Shiva\nHow have the ideals of the wrestler's body changed in contemporary India? Once distinct\, the worlds of traditional wrestling and modern fitness culture are increasingly intersecting. Gyms now incorporate training methods rooted in the akhara\, while wrestlers present themselves through the visual language of bodybuilding and social media\, cultivating physiques that are leaner\, more muscular\, and shaped by new forms of visibility.\n\nIn this conversation\, anthropologist Michiel Baas and filmmaker Paromita Vohra explore the changing aesthetics of the body\, tracing how ideas of strength\, masculinity\, discipline\, and aspiration are being reimagined across wrestling\, fitness culture\, and digital media. Drawing on Baas's research\, the discussion considers what these shifting bodily practices reveal about contemporary India and the evolving relationship between tradition\, performance\, and identity.\n\nThis event is in conjunction with programming for Grip\, Hold\, Pin.
DTEND:20260816T103000Z
DTSTART:20260816T093000Z
SUMMARY:Muscular India
UID:1373@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/muscular-india/
